  "title": "Qualia: The Feel of Experience",
  "emoji": "💭",
  "color": "#581C87",
  "description": "One-minute skill: Qualia: The Feel of Experience.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Does she know what red looks like?",
      "visual": "🖤"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Qualia are the subjective, felt qualities of experience: the redness of red, the painfulness of pain, the sweetness of sugar.",
      "visual": "🍬"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Frank Jackson argued that when the scientist finally sees red, she learns something new, proving qualia go beyond physical facts.",
      "visual": "🔴"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Physicalists counter she does not learn a new fact.",
      "visual": "🤹"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Whether qualia are real properties of the world or useful fictions generated by the brain remains one of the most heated debates.",
      "visual": "🔥"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "You know exactly what pain feels like. That gap is the mystery of qualia.",
      "visual": "🤷"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What are qualia?",
    "options": [
      "The subjective felt qualities of experience",
      "Chemical signals between neurons",
      "The physical structure of sensory organs"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
